, -0.00830078125, -0.0265960693359375, -0.02349853515625, -0.004772186279296875, -0.017669677734375, 0.04437255859375, 0.0156707763671875, 0.0232391357421875, 0.02777099609375, 0.01139068603515625, 0.00905609130859375, 0.003662109375, -0.048614501953125, 0.00439453125, -0.0072174072265625, 0.0007915496826171875, 0.056732177734375, -0.0167236328125, -0.0006508827209472656, -0.01207733154296875, 0.0034332275390625, -0.0021686553955078125, 0.0267486572265625, 0.00860595703125, 0.0029296875, 0.0031490325927734375, 0.01160430908203125, -0.01399993896484375, 0.0246734619140625, 0.002483367919921875, 0.021728515625, -0.0005254745483398438, 0.012542724609375, 0.0115814208984375, -0.014556884765625, 0.004192352294921875, -0.00928497314453125, -0.009124755859375, -0.0193328857421875, -0.01132965087890625, 0.027801513671875, -0.036590576171875, 0.0146636962890625, 0.0084686279296875, 0.01611328125, -0.0163116455078125, -0.002384185791015625, -0.0172119140625, 0.014404296875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Introduction\n\nHow level-0 players behave and how they are perceived by higher-level players are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. In level-k models, \"level-0 actions\" refer to choices made by players who do not engage in strategic reasoning; these actions are typically assumed to be non-strategic, such as random selection or choosing salient or focal options. In contrast, \"level-0 beliefs\" are the expectations that these players hold about the actions of others, which are also presumed to be non-strategic and uninformed by higher-order reasoning. Throughout this paper, we define level-0 actions as the actual choices made without strategic adjustment, and level-0 beliefs as the stated expectations about others\u2019 choices, elicited prior to any strategic reasoning or adjustment. For example, a level-0 belief might be expressed as, \"I think other teams will just pick the middle number,\" or \"Others will probably choose randomly.\" We emphasize that, in our operationalization, both actions and beliefs are intended to capture non-strategic, uninformed reasoning, and we take care to distinguish between the two in our analysis and reporting. To ensure clarity in classification, we developed systematic coding criteria (see Methods).\n\nUnderstanding the formation and alignment of level-0 actions and beliefs is crucial for accurately modeling behavior in strategic settings. This study addresses two main research questions: (1) How are level-0 actions and beliefs formed, and do they align within individuals? (2) How do these patterns differ across games, and what are the implications for the assumptions underlying standard level-k models? We hypothesize that the alignment between level-0 actions and beliefs is context-dependent and that standard level-k assumptions may not hold uniformly across different strategic settings.\n\nMethods\n\nOverview and Rationale for Team-Based Consensus\n\nWe employ the intra-team communication method developed by Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), in which participants are assigned to teams and required to reach consensus on both their action and belief. The team-based consensus format was chosen for two reasons. First, it allows for the observation and recording of explicit reasoning processes, as team members must articulate and negotiate their reasoning aloud, providing rich data for transcript analysis. Second, this format enables us to examine how group deliberation shapes the formation and alignment of level-0 actions and beliefs, which is directly relevant to our research questions about the robustness and perception of non-strategic reasoning. While we acknowledge that group dynamics may influence outcomes, the team-based approach offers unique insights into the social construction of level-0 reasoning that are not accessible through individual elicitation alone. To address potential confounds from group dynamics such as dominance or conformity, we recommend future studies incorporate methodological controls such as requiring private, written responses from each participant before group discussion, or including an individual elicitation condition for comparison.\n\nExperimental Design and Procedure\n\nParticipants were recruited from a university subject pool and randomly assigned to teams of three using a computerized randomization procedure that ensured no prior acquaintances were grouped together. This resulted in 20 teams per game (40 teams total). Each team played one of two experimental games: the canonical beauty contest game or a variant of the 11-20 game. In total, 120 undergraduate students (60 per game) participated. Demographic information was collected (majority aged 18\u201325, balanced gender representation), and randomization was checked to confirm that teams were balanced across age and gender.\n\nUpon arrival, participants received standardized written and verbal instructions explaining the rules of the assigned game. Instructions emphasized that teams would need to reach a consensus on both their action (the number to choose) and their belief (what number they expected other teams to choose). Teams were given up to 15 minutes to discuss openly and reach consensus, with discussions audio-recorded for subsequent analysis. No time pressure was imposed, but teams were encouraged to ensure all members contributed. After consensus was reached, teams recorded their final action and belief on a response sheet. To incentivize thoughtful participation, teams received monetary rewards based on their performance in the game: in the beauty contest, the team whose choice was closest to two-thirds of the average won a prize; in the 11-20 game variant, teams received a doubled reward if their number was exactly one less than another team's choice, and a small bonus for choosing the lowest unique number. Additionally, teams received a bonus if their stated belief was within 2 units of the actual modal choice of other teams.\n\nGame Descriptions\n\nBeauty Contest Game\n\nThe beauty contest game requires each team to choose a number between 0 and 100, aiming to guess two-thirds of the average number chosen by all teams. This game is widely regarded as a benchmark for eliciting non-strategic (level-0) behavior due to its simple structure and clear focal point (50), making it suitable for examining the alignment of level-0 actions and beliefs.\n\n11-20 Game Variant\n\nThe 11-20 game, originally introduced by Goeree et al. (2018), involves each team selecting an integer between 11 and 20, with payoffs awarded if a team's chosen number is exactly one less than another team's choice. In our variant, we doubled the reward for achieving this condition and added a small bonus for choosing the lowest unique number. These modifications were made to increase the incentive for strategic reasoning and to introduce additional focal points, thereby testing whether level-0 reasoning persists or changes under more complex and competitive incentives.\n\nThe two games were selected to provide contrasting contexts for level-0 reasoning: the beauty contest game offers a clear focal point and minimal strategic complexity, while the modified 11-20 game introduces more complex incentives and multiple potential focal points. By comparing these settings, we aim to test the robustness of standard level-0 assumptions and to explore whether context-dependent shifts in non-strategic reasoning occur.\n\nTranscript Coding and Reliability\n\nAll team discussions were transcribed and coded to identify instances of level-0 reasoning. The coding scheme was developed through an iterative process: first, we reviewed prior literature to identify common definitions and examples of level-0 reasoning; second, we conducted pilot coding on a subset of transcripts to refine operational criteria; third, we created a coding manual with explicit decision rules and examples. Level-0 reasoning was defined as explicit statements or arguments indicating non-strategic choice (e.g., \"just pick the middle number,\" \"choose randomly,\" \"pick the lowest number because it stands out\") and corresponding beliefs about others' choices that reflect a lack of strategic adjustment (e.g., \"others will probably pick randomly,\" \"I think most teams will just pick 50\"). \n\nTo systematically distinguish between non-strategic and minimally strategic reasoning, coders followed a decision tree: (1) Does the statement reference only salient, focal, or random choices, with no mention of anticipating others' strategies? If yes, code as level-0. (2) Does the statement reference what others might do, but without any reasoning about others' possible strategic adjustments? If yes, code as level-0 belief. (3) If the statement includes any reference to anticipating or countering others' strategies, code as strategic (not level-0). Ambiguous cases\u2014such as statements that could be interpreted as either non-strategic or minimally strategic (e.g., \"maybe 15 is a good number because it's not too high or too low\")\u2014were discussed by coders and only classified as level-0 if there was clear evidence of non-strategic intent and no reference to anticipating others\u2019 strategies. For example, \"If we pick 12, maybe others will go for 13,\" was not coded as level-0 unless accompanied by explicit statements of non-strategic reasoning.\n\nTwo independent coders, trained on a subset of transcripts and blind to the experimental condition, reviewed all transcripts. Discrepancies were resolved through discussion until consensus was reached; in cases where consensus could not be reached, a third coder adjudicated. Inter-rater reliability, measured by Cohen\u2019s kappa, was 0.87, indicating high agreement. The coding manual and decision tree are available upon request.\n\nResults\n\nIn the beauty contest game, 18% of teams (4 out of 20) exhibited level-0 reasoning based on transcript coding. Within these teams, the mean chosen number was 50 (SD = 2.1), and their stated beliefs about others\u2019 choices also centered around 50 (mean = 49.8, SD = 2.4). Given the very small number of teams classified as level-0 (n=4), these statistics are reported for descriptive purposes only and should not be interpreted as precise estimates or subjected to inferential analysis. The close correspondence between actions and beliefs suggests alignment in non-strategic reasoning for these teams.\n\nIn the 11-20 game variant, 15% of teams (3 out of 20) were classified as exhibiting level-0 reasoning. For these teams, the mean chosen number was 15.2 (SD = 2.8), while their stated beliefs about others\u2019 choices averaged 17.6 (SD = 1.9). Again, due to the very small sample size (n=3), these statistics are purely descriptive and should not be over-interpreted. Here, a notable divergence between actions and beliefs was observed: teams tended to choose lower numbers themselves but expected others to choose higher numbers. The observed means also differed from the standard level-0 assumption of choosing the lowest available number (11).\n\nDiscussion\n\nThe observed divergence between beliefs and actions in the 11-20 game variant is significant for level-k modeling because it challenges the assumption that level-0 behavior is both non-strategic and commonly understood by higher-level players. Our findings suggest that level-0 reasoning may be influenced by contextual cues and payoff structures, and that higher-level players may misperceive or mispredict level-0 behavior in certain games. The team-based consensus format, while providing valuable data on explicit reasoning, may also have influenced the results"}}